  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Monday) \nIssued at 357 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAs all the aforementioned shortwaves eventually spill into the \nwestern Caribbean, they'll find a more unstable region to initiate \nand organize convection. At the same time, the upper trough will \nbecome more positively tilted as the core of the main longwave \ntrough moves northeast and a wave breaking pattern sets up with the \nlatest shortwave starting to cutoff over FL and the SW Atlantic. \nThis will create a pathway for convection to gain latitude out of \nthe Caribbean and enter the Bahamas. Traditional 500mb ensemble \ncluster analysis is primarily focusing on other sources of variance \nin determining extended range scenarios, and this is likely due to \nour focus area being on the edge of the domain and focused primarily \nabove 500mb. However, we can still identify a few scenarios from the \nclusters to clue us into the fact that where this upper-low cuts off \nwill have an implication on QPF forecasts this weekend and into next \nweek. Thankfully WPC QPF clusters can at least shed light on what \nthe potential impacts may be depending on the evolution of the upper \npattern. What we can say is that the east coast metros will be the \ntarget of heavy rainfall over the weekend. What's uncertain is just \nhow much and exactly when. Generally speaking, there's a lower \nQPF scenario that on average brings 1-2\" of rain to much of the \nsoutheast coast of Florida, then there are multiple more impactful\nscenarios that differ on placement of the highest precip amounts \nacross the east coast from Cape Canaveral down to Homestead. The \nhigher end scenarios generally agree on amounts between 5-7\" over \nthe 48 hour period this weekend, with the potential for scattered \nto isolated locations receiving somewhere near double those \namounts. For this reason we remain in a Slight Risk (15%) for \nflash flooding in WPCs Day 5 Excessive Rainfall Outlook. Winds and\ngusts (non- thunderstorm) will also be a concern over the weekend\nas the gradient between convection in the Bahamas and an \napproaching front tightens. There's currently a high probability \nof sustained winds in the 25-30 mph range across east and west \ncoast metros, with a moderate chance of gusts exceeding 40 mph. \nThe main window for these high winds will be on Sunday. The wet \npattern will likely continue into next week as the northern stream\nfrontal system and cutoff low interact and eventually dissipate \nor advect out of the region.",
